1
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:11,600
Every 30 seconds of a night's sleep gets a label:
Wake, N1, N2, N3 or REM. Experts score them by hand.

2
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:17,100
Can a computer read sleep from one EEG channel, a single pair of scalp electrodes?

3
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:23,400
One pipeline. Input: a single Fpz-Cz EEG channel, 100 samples a second, all night.

4
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:29,600
Process: cut into 30-second epochs, make a frequency picture of each, then a CNN and a BiGRU.

5
00:00:30,300 --> 00:00:33,900
Output: one of five stages for every 30 seconds, a hypnogram.

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,900
A 2026 paper in Scientific Reports reports about 83% accuracy from one channel, chaining four ideas.

7
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:46,700
We set out to reproduce it. Within our time and compute, we could not.

8
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:52,700
The paper splits epochs at random, so one person can be on both sides. We split by person.

9
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:59,800
Why? A new patient is a new person. A random split can
reward recognising the person, not reading the sleep.

10
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:08,100
So we changed the question: does it still work on a person
the model has never seen, and can we explain every step?

11
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:15,900
Scope: not a replication or clinical validation. Twenty people, one public dataset, unseen people only.

12
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:23,200
Hypothesis, formed after the baseline failed: nights differ
in signal strength, so per-night scaling should help.

13
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:32,900
Our data is Sleep-EDF, public on PhysioNet: healthy adults recorded
at home on cassette recorders, usually two nights of about 20 hours.

14
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:41,000
Trained technicians scored every 30-second page of each whole
night by hand, following the 1968 Rechtschaffen and Kales rules.

15
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:46,700
Two experts can disagree, so these labels are a trusted reference, not perfect truth.

16
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:54,300
We use 20 people and 39 nights: 42,307 epochs. Trimmed
to its scored span, a night is about 1,085 epochs, or 9 hours.

17
00:01:55,300 --> 00:02:02,200
Three variables: the EEG signal, the stage label, and a person
ID that only splits the data and never feeds the model.

18
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:09,800
Limits: healthy adults only, just 20 people, human-scored
labels and one channel. This data cannot speak for patients.

19
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:18,300
STFT: a 2-second Hamming window slides in 1-second steps,
turning each epoch into a 129 × 29 picture of frequency over time.

20
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:25,600
Why frequencies? Sleep stages are defined by rhythms: slow
waves mark N3, spindles N2, alpha waves relaxed wake.

21
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:34,100
The paper uses a synchrosqueezed transform. We chose STFT because
we can draw, compute and check it by hand. We never compared them.

22
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:42,300
CNN: three blocks of 32, 64 and 128 channels squeeze each
picture into 128 numbers. Its limit: it sees one epoch at a time.

23
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:49,300
BiGRU: two layers read 20 neighbouring epochs
forwards and backwards, because sleep is a sequence.

24
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,500
Why? Sleep comes in long runs, so an epoch is easier to judge with its neighbours than alone.

25
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,800
The cost: it uses future epochs, so it works offline only, never in real time.

26
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:08,700
Contrastive learning trains the CNN's embedding: epochs
that should match are pulled together, the rest pushed apart.

27
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:14,900
It adds a second training phase with many settings to tune, so it costs more time and compute.

28
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:22,200
We dropped the paper's contrastive stage: reproducing it
failed, and we wanted a chain we can check step by step.

29
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:26,800
We never ran that comparison, so we do not claim it helps or hurts.

30
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:34,800
Testing is by person: ten folds, with 16 people training, 2
choosing the checkpoint and 2 tested. Nobody appears twice.

31
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:39,100
Only two test people per fold, so scores swing between folds.

32
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:46,700
Baseline first: macro-F1 56.42%, 95% interval 48.26 to 63.44.
Accuracy 65.00%. Folds swing from 39.95% to 74.39%.

33
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:53,600
Opening single nights, three were right on only 7.22%,
18.60% and 19.01% of epochs. The average hid them.

34
00:03:54,600 --> 00:04:01,200
The baseline already z-scored each frequency, but with
one ruler: the mean and spread of the 16 training people.

35
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:07,200
R1 gives every night its own ruler: that night's own mean and spread, with no labels used.

36
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:15,300
Same architecture, retrained: macro-F1 74.86%, interval 71.33
to 78.03; accuracy 80.99%. Gain +18.44, interval 10.07 to 27.55.

37
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,600
The three failing nights recover to 82.54%, 88.68% and 84.87%.

38
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:27,500
But R1 reads each test night's unlabeled signal and was
chosen after the baseline failed. It is offline and post-hoc.

39
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:36,600
Whole-recording statistics give 71.84%: −3.02 versus R1, interval −7.12
to +1.22. It crosses zero: no clear difference, not proof of equality.

40
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:46,300
N1, the lightest sleep, stays weak: R1 recovers 44.69% of true N1 and
38.91% of its N1 calls are right. Three fixes on validation were all rejected.

41
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:54,200
What we can say: in these 20 people an average can hide
failing nights, and per-night scaling helped in this test.

42
00:04:55,300 --> 00:05:01,100
What we cannot say: that it works on new people, since R1 is offline and was chosen after the fact.

43
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:07,700
Nor that 80.99% beats or matches the paper's 83%, that it is clinically ready, or that N1 is solved.

44
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:12,000
None of the 78 audited participants is certified independent.

45
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:20,100
Nights differ, and an average can hide the one that fails.
Next: lock the protocol, then test on truly independent data.
